/*
time O(26·n) = O(n) in practice, because Collections.max(m.values()) scans at most 26 characters.
space O(1) because the map holds at most 26 characters (constant).
You’re sliding a window [l…r] over the string.
Inside that window, you want the substring to become all the same character by replacing at most k characters.

To check if the window is valid, you ask:

How many characters do I need to replace?
→ window_size - count_of_most_frequent_char_in_window

If this number is > k, the window is invalid → shrink from the left.

If it’s ≤ k → window is valid → update the longest length.
*/



class Solution {
    public int characterReplacement(String s, int k) {
        int n = s.length();
        Map<Character,Integer> m = new HashMap();
        int l=0;
        int res = 0;
        for(int r=0;r<n;r++){
            m.put(s.charAt(r),m.getOrDefault(s.charAt(r),0)+1);
            while( r-l+1 - Collections.max(m.values()) > k ){
                m.put(s.charAt(l),m.get(s.charAt(l))-1);
                l++;
            }
            res = Math.max( res, r-l+1);

        }
        return res;
    }
}
